Rational-basis review of tax classifications is exceptionally deferential. A state may favor neighborhood continuity and protect owners who planned around a known, slowly rising levy; new buyers can price the tax into their decision. Those aims are legitimate, and an acquisition-value rule with a capped annual adjustment is a rational, if crude, means to them. Parent-child continuity is likewise a permissible family-stability choice, not a forbidden lineage caste. Allegheny Pittsburgh is distinguishable: there the assessor defied a state-law command of current-value uniformity. Here the disparity is the statute, applied evenhandedly. Gross unfairness does not make a tax classification unconstitutional when no suspect class or fundamental right is involved. Courts do not sit to recalibrate fiscal equity.

Separately, the system is poor policy. It produces large, permanent horizontal inequities among owners of similar homes, discourages mobility, favors incumbents without regard to need, and starves local services as the tax base lags market values. Voters may prefer that bargain; legislatures should not.

