The Court has long held that under rational basis review, a state’s taxing scheme must be upheld if it rests on any reasonably conceivable legitimate state interest. Proposition 13’s acquisition-value system is not an aberration from California law; it is the deliberately chosen policy of the State’s own constitution. The State could rationally seek to protect existing homeowners from unaffordable tax spikes caused by rising property values, thereby preserving neighborhood stability and preventing displacement of long-time residents who planned their finances around a predictable tax burden. The system also provides certainty for prospective buyers, who know the tax consequences before purchasing. While the parent-child exception and the uneven benefits may be coarse and open to criticism, rational basis review does not require a perfect fit or wise policy—only a plausible relationship to a legitimate end. The disparity here is not the result of an assessor’s unauthorized and arbitrary departure from a uniform-assessment mandate, as in Allegheny Pittsburgh, but the uniform operation of a statewide constitutional rule. That deliberate choice does not render it irrational. Reasonable minds can debate the fairness of Proposition 13, but the Equal Protection Clause does not authorize courts to substitute their judgment for that of the voters. I would therefore uphold the scheme.

As a matter of policy, the acquisition-value system is in my view deeply flawed. It creates enormous horizontal inequities among owners of similar property, imposes disproportionate burdens on younger and less affluent buyers, discourages mobility by “locking in” homeowners to their current residences to preserve their low tax bases, and artificially constrains housing supply. These distortions undermine both fairness and economic efficiency. However, the policy’s shortcomings do not make it unconstitutional under well-established rational basis doctrine.

