Under rational basis review, our role is not to assess whether a tax system achieves an optimal distribution of burdens, but whether it serves a plausible public purpose without creating arbitrary harm. California’s acquisition-value system seeks to protect homeowners—especially those on fixed incomes—from being forced out of their homes by escalating property values. Preventing displacement and fostering neighborhood stability directly advances human welfare by providing security and predictability. Given that judicial invalidation of this decade-old framework would inflict widespread economic shock, housing insecurity, and administrative chaos on millions of households, the net societal consequences favor judicial restraint. The system easily satisfies the low threshold of rational basis review. The law is constitutional.

As a matter of public policy, however, the measure generates severe net harm. By dramatically divorcing tax liabilities from actual market value, the system creates profound market distortions. It traps long-time owners in homes they might otherwise vacate—stifling mobility and reducing housing inventory—while shifting a disproportionate, punitive tax burden onto younger, newer buyers who provide critical labor and vitality to the economy. Furthermore, the resulting suppression of tax revenues has starved essential public goods, including public education and infrastructure, diminishing the broader welfare and flourishing of the state. While shielding vulnerable residents from displacement is a worthy objective, achieving it through blunt, perpetual property-tax freezes that favor lineage and tenure over need ultimately degrades overall economic dynamism and societal well-being. Targeted relief programs would achieve the humanitarian goal without incurring such staggering social costs.

