Under rational basis review, our role is not to assess legislative wisdom, but to determine whether a policy plausibly advances legitimate ends without creating arbitrary harm. California possesses a legitimate interest in fostering neighborhood stability and protecting residents from being forced from their homes by sudden, unaffordable property tax spikes. Safeguarding settled reliance interests and preventing displacement contributes to societal stability. Because the voters deliberately enacted this structure, displacing it through judicial mandate would generate widespread disruption and erode trust in democratic processes. The classification survives rational basis review and must be upheld.

Separately, the acquisition-value system is profoundly bad public policy. By heavily penalizing relocation, it creates severe market distortions, locking people into unsuitable homes and drastically choking the supply of residential housing. Moreover, it creates vast generational inequities, shifting the tax burden onto younger and newer residents while starving municipalities and public schools of the stable revenue necessary to maximize communal well-being. The aggregate societal welfare lost to housing shortages, labor market friction, and underfunded public goods vastly outweighs the localized security provided to long-term homeowners.

